# Release lines (ENG-2110)

Read before touching `versions/`, `release/`, `scripts-release/`, a `release-*.yml` workflow,
`pr-checks.yml`, `build-deb.yml`, `sdk-build.yml`, `hw-smoke.yml`, `.github/sdk.env`, `build.sh`,
`nfpm.yaml`, `crossbuild/`, or anything that decides what version a build reports. This is the
contract the repo files encode. The human runbooks are `docs/release-lines.md` and
`docs/release-admin-setup.md`. The model is the ds-tegra-distro release-line process (its
`.ai/release-lines.md` and `docs/release-lines.md`); this repo runs the same jobs ported to one
product, one repository, and a deb built against the exported SDK instead of images. The ds
monorepo carries the same port; `scripts-release/` is byte-identical in both, and only
`release/config.json`, `release/matrix.json` and the build workflow differ.

## The rule this port exists for

Nothing in this repository's CI acts on another repository. The build reads (this checkout and
its private submodules `schemas` and `ds-design-system` with `REPO_ACCESS_TOKEN`, the SDK
installer from S3; `hw-smoke.yml` reads `ds-sensor-portal-tests` the same way; never a push,
tag, dispatch or PR elsewhere), and every `actions/create-github-app-token` step has exactly
`repositories: ${{ github.event.repository.name }}`. `pr-checks.yml` fails a PR that names
`meta-dsensos` or `ds-tegra-distro` in a `release-*.yml` or in `scripts-release/`, that
dispatches into another repo, that widens a token, or whose `build-deb.yml` or `sdk-build.yml`
dispatches, mints an App token or writes to a repository. A portal release reaches an OS image
only through the OS layer's own pin bump (`SENSOR_PORTAL_REV` and `SENSOR_PORTAL_BRANCH` in
`conf/distro/include/ds-src-pins.inc`, its `bump-app-pin.yml` workflow or a person), then the
lockfile bump in ds-tegra-distro. App lines and OS lines are independent: an OS line pins
whichever app line it chooses (`main`, `N.x.x`, `N.M.x`), and this repo follows an OS line only
for its SDK (`.github/sdk.env`, below). The OS repos' lockstep lines, `base.yml` branch names,
lockfile pins and the sync job have no counterpart here and must not grow one.

## Lines, tags, versions

- `release/config.json`: `{"products": ["portal"], "product_prefix": false, "bookkeeping_files":
  [".github/sdk.env"], "artifact": {"kind": "debs", "debs": ["ds-sensor-portal"], "deb_arch":
  "arm64", "deb_version_rule": "deb-version"}, "version_probe": {"deb": "ds-sensor-portal",
  "path": "./opt/ds/sensor-portal/ds-sensor-portal", "pattern": "v{version}"}}` (the product is
  `portal`: `version.py` rejects a hyphen). `artifact.kind` is `debs` or `install-trees` and the
  other keys depend on it (`services` and `info_file` with a probe without `deb` under
  `install-trees`, which is ds's shape; the two key sets never mix, and the old top-level deb
  keys are refused). `version.py config` prints it with defaults, `version.py check` validates it
  together with `versions/` and `release/matrix.json` (`{"products": {"portal": {"flavors":
  ["portal_portal-arm64_release"]}}}`, flavors are `<product>_<machine>_<type>` and the machine
  token is the S3 prefix, not the SDK MACHINE).
- With `product_prefix: false` (this repo) the lines are `main` (major), `N.x.x` (minor, cut from
  tag `vN.0.0`), `N.M.x` (patch, cut from `vN.M.0`). `scripts-release/lines.py parse` classifies a
  name; anything with a `/` (`dev/main`, `release/vl`) or like `1foo.x.x` is kind `none` and the
  rc-build `plan` job skips it. Parent of `N.M.x` is `N.x.x` when that branch exists, else `main`;
  parent of `N.x.x` is `main`. The code keeps the prefixed mode of the reference working so the
  copies of the tooling stay one code base; nothing here uses it.
- Tags: release `vX.Y.Z` (a GitHub release on it), RC pre-release `rc/<sha7>`, one per green
  push. The old hand-made `v*` tags are history; immutable releases, once on, burn a deleted
  release's tag name, so creating jobs default `dry_run=true`.
- `versions/portal` is the pending version: the version the next promote on this branch tags, one
  `X.Y.Z` line, written only by the jobs with `[skip ci]` (create-branch's opening commit, the
  post-promote bump). Promote on `main` tags the pending version and bumps the major
  (`AUTO_LEVEL`, unchanged from the reference); on `N.x.x` the minor; on `N.M.x` the patch.
  `bump=major|minor|patch` overrides. After `v3.0.0` the pending on `main` is `4.0.0`; minor
  releases come from a `3.x.x` line.
- Version strings: dev `X.Y.Z-dev.<N>.g<sha7>[.dirty]` (every build not given a version), RC
  `X.Y.Z-rc.<N>.github-<sha7>` (`N = git rev-list --count`, so RCs of one version order), release
  `X.Y.Z`. Lowercase, never `+`. Deb `Version` fields take the dpkg form from `version.py
  deb-version`: the first `-` becomes `~`, later ones `.` (`1.1.0~rc.5.github.abc1234` sorts
  before `1.1.0`, as SemVer intends; a plain `-` would make the RC sort after the release).
- Into the binary and the deb: `scripts-ci/build-version.sh` (the same file as ds's) prints
  `DS_VERSION`, `DS_DEB_VERSION`, `DS_RELEASE_LINE` and `GIT_HASH`. A non-empty `DS_BUILD_VERSION`
  (set by `build-deb.yml` for an RC or a release, passed through `crossbuild/run.sh -e` on the
  developer route) wins, with `DS_RELEASE_LINE` beside it. Otherwise the tag rule: an exact
  `vX.Y.Z` tag on a clean HEAD gives `X.Y.Z`, anything else gives the dev string from
  `versions/portal` and git. `build.sh` SDK mode evals the script and stamps
  `handlers.GitTag=v<DS_VERSION>` and `GitBranch=<DS_RELEASE_LINE, else SENSOR_PORTAL_BRANCH,
  else main>` (the sidebar `version-portal` and `-version`); `build-deb.yml` and
  `crossbuild/container-build.sh` run `NFPM_VERSION=<DS_DEB_VERSION> nfpm pkg` over `nfpm.yaml`
  (`version_schema: none`), so the deb is `ds-sensor-portal_<deb version>_arm64.deb`. On an image
  build the OS layer's `ds-versioning` class computes the same string from the pinned tree (the
  same tag rule, so a pinned release commit stamps `X.Y.Z` everywhere) and sets the recipe's
  ldflags and `PKGV`. `git describe` is read nowhere.

## Builds and the S3 layout

`.github/workflows/build-deb.yml` (`sdk-build.md` has the step list) is the build: `workflow_call`
with `flavor`, `build_version`, `release_line`, `sdk_uri`, `sdk_sha256`, `sdk_os_release` and the
output `build_prefix`; `workflow_dispatch` with the same minus `flavor`. Every input is read as
`inputs.*`. `pr-checks.yml` and the release workflows call it and own the concurrency. On a lab
runner (`[self-hosted, Linux, X64, Ubuntu]`, the flasher AWS identity) it checks out the PR head
(not GitHub's merge commit) with the submodules over `REPO_ACCESS_TOKEN` and `fetch-depth: 0`,
resolves the SDK (`scripts/sdk-resolve.sh` over `.github/sdk.env`), ingests it
(`scripts/sdk-ingest.sh` into `$RUNNER_TEMP`), builds natively in `build.sh`'s SDK mode with
`DS_BUILD_VERSION`/`DS_RELEASE_LINE` when given, packages with the pinned nfpm, zips the web-asset
tree as `build-artifacts.zip` (with `swagger/`, never `docs/`), then `scripts-release/artifacts.py
build-manifest --kind debs` writes `manifest.json` and `SHA256SUMS` (last) over the deb and the
zip, the deb's `Version` and the `v<version>` string in the binary are checked when a version was
requested, and everything is copied to `s3://ds-yocto-artifacts/portal-arm64/<UTC>-<run_id>-standard/`
with `SHA256SUMS` last. The SDK install is removed at the end. No Docker in CI: `crossbuild/run.sh`
(the SDK image, or the bullseye image for a JetPack 5 unit) is the developer's local route.

The deb is SDK-only: it links against the dsensos sysroot and runs on dsensos units. Releases no
longer serve JetPack 5 units.

`manifest.json` (schema 1): `job_id`, `build_datetime`, `buildparams.{flavor, product, machine,
hardened, build_type, build_version, release_line, sdk: {uri, sha256, machine, os_release}}`,
`commit_hashes[]` (first entry `name` is the repo name, `sensor-portal`, then `dsensos-sdk` = the
installer's sha256), `run_url`, `artifacts.debs[]` (`key`, `package`, `version`, `architecture`,
`sha256`, `bytes`; keys `deb/<file>`), `artifacts.extra[]` (the zip). `buildparams.sdk` says
which toolchain compiled the build; `os_release` is the OS release it came from (`vX.Y.Z`),
`pinned` for the explicit pin, or the caller's value when a release build reuses its RC's SDK.
The bucket is shared with the OS builds, so S3 keys keep a product segment
(`rc/portal/<sha7>/release-manifest.json`, `release/portal/vX.Y.Z/...`) while git tags do not.

`.github/workflows/sdk-build.yml` is `workflow_dispatch` only (the same resolve, ingest and
`build.sh --out`, uploading the install tree for a check against the image); it has no
`push`/`pull_request` trigger and builds no deb.

`hw-smoke.yml` (workflow_call `release_tag`, `blocking`; workflow_dispatch `release_tag`) is the
old `build.yml` test job on its own: it downloads a release's deb, installs it on the shared test
sensor (`vars.SENSOR_TESTS_IP_ADDRESS`) and runs the `ds-sensor-portal-tests` smoke suite. It
runs from `release-staging-deploy.yml` after a promote with `blocking: false` (a failure is a
warning until ENG-2139), never on a pull request. The deb being SDK-only, the test sensor has to
run dsensos. `ui-tests.yml` (go test, Playwright, hosted) keeps its own triggers and is not a
required check.

## The SDK: `.github/sdk.env` is bookkeeping

`.github/sdk.env` names the OS product and release line this app line follows (`SDK_OS_PRODUCT`,
`SDK_OS_LINE`: `N.x.x`, `N.M.x` or `latest`) and the SDK MACHINE, or an explicit installer pin
(`SDK_S3_URI` + `SDK_SHA256`, in force until the first OS release ships an SDK). The build
resolves the newest shipped OS release on the line that carries an SDK for the machine at build
time (`scripts/sdk-resolve.sh`, `sdk-build.md`), so the file never changes when the OS ships a
release: it changes only when this app line moves to another OS line. It is the
`bookkeeping_files` entry of `release/config.json`, with the side effect that entails: a commit
touching only `versions/` and `sdk.env` counts as bookkeeping for `release_git.py unmerged`
(merge-up keeps the target's copy, promote does not see it as unmerged work, abandon does not
refuse over it). A release build does not resolve: promote's `check` job reads `builds[].sdk`
from the RC's `release-manifest.json` and passes `sdk_uri`/`sdk_sha256`/`sdk_os_release` per
flavor, and the gate refuses a release whose SDK differs from the RC's. The pilot and `main`
follow `vertex` `2.x.x`.

## The jobs

Every job is a workflow file; `gh workflow run <file> --ref <line>`. Control jobs run on the GKE
runner `yocto-spot` (`DS_AWS_EXPECTED_ROLE: gke-embedded-yocto-build`, `scripts-ci/aws-preflight.sh`
first); the ship job on `yocto-ondemand` (never reclaimed mid-copy); the build on the lab
runners. Prologue: `check-tools.sh` (gh, git, jq, python3, aws, plus dpkg-deb for the debs kind
or tar for install-trees), then the App token when `vars.DS_RELEASE_APP_CLIENT_ID` is set, else
`GITHUB_TOKEN` with `TOKEN_KIND=github` (a real run then stops before any push with `lib.sh
no_app_hint` naming the manual command). No `register` jobs: `main` is the default branch, so
every file is dispatchable.

| Workflow | Trigger / inputs | Jobs and what they do |
|---|---|---|
| `release-rc-build.yml` | push to `main`, `[0-9]*.x.x`, `[0-9]*.[0-9]*.x`; dispatch (no inputs, rerun of the tip); concurrency `<workflow>-<sha>` | `plan` (`plan.py`: skip, product, kind, line, rc_version, pending, matrix) → `build` (matrix over the one flavor, `uses: build-deb.yml` with the RC version and the line; the SDK from `sdk.env`) → `rc-gate` (`rc-gate.sh`, S3 rediscovery by run id) → `publish-rc` (`publish-rc.sh`: draft → assets → pre-release `rc/<sha7>`, S3 index) . Merge-up is not a job here (ENG-2197, below) |
| `release-promote.yml` | dispatch `bump`, `merge_children`, `dry_run` | `resolve` → `check` (`promote.sh --check`: tip == dispatched sha, green RC on the tip through `walk-versions-only`, children merged via `unmerged`, `promote-plan`, tag absent; concurrency `release-line`; then "The RC's SDK": the RC's `release-manifest.json` asset → `build_matrix` with `sdk_*` per flavor, kept as the `rc-manifest` artifact) → `build` (release version, `uses: build-deb.yml` with the RC's SDK) → `release-gate` (`rc-gate.sh --kind release --rc-manifest`) → `publish` (`promote.sh --publish`: tag by API, draft → assets → pre-release `vX.Y.Z (staging)` whose notes name each build's SDK, S3 index, one-file bump commit pushed 3 attempts) → `staging-deploy` |
| `release-ship.yml` | dispatch `version`, `dry_run`; concurrency `release-ship` | `plan` (`ship.sh --plan`, dry run ends here) → `ship` (`environment: production`; copies every object to `release/portal/vX.Y.Z/portal-arm64/standard/<file>`, reads back, `release.json`, flips to the full release) |
| `release-create-branch.yml` | dispatch `version`, `kind`, `seed_from`, `dry_run`; concurrency `release-line` | `create-branch.sh`: line at the tag or at `seed_from` (the tag must be its ancestor), opening commit touching only `versions/portal` |
| `release-merge-up.yml` | `workflow_run` on "Release RC build" completed, branches `[0-9]*.x.x`, `[0-9]*.[0-9]*.x` (child = `workflow_run.head_branch`); dispatch `child`, `pr`, `dry_run`; `run-name` `Merge-up <child>` | `resolve` (on `workflow_run` only a `success` conclusion with the App; skips `main`, a non-line, or an RC sha the child moved past, `walk-versions-only`; parent via `lines.py`) → `merge` (`merge-up.sh`: green child tip or `CALLER_SHA`/`CALLER_RUN` from the triggering run, worktree `merge --no-ff`, `release_git.py merge-keep-target` keeps the parent's `versions/` and `.github/sdk.env`, conflict branch `merge-up/<child>/<sha7>` = the parent plus the merge with the markers committed, + PR, `--pr` finish after `pr-check` and a no-markers-left check); `merge-up-skipped` (green RC, no App: the manual dispatch as a notice) |
| `release-abandon.yml` | dispatch `line`, `force`, `dry_run` | `resolve` (refuses `main`) → `abandon` (`abandon.sh`: refuses unmerged work or children unless `force`, deletes the ref, `prune-rc.sh --line`: RC releases, tags and S3 objects, keeping any sha a `v*` tag points at) |
| `release-staging-deploy.yml` | workflow_call `request_json` | `record` (the request as an artifact, outputs the tag) → `smoke` (uses `hw-smoke.yml` on the promoted release, non-blocking) |
| `pr-checks.yml` | every PR; dispatch | `build` (uses `build-deb.yml`, dev version), `release-tooling` (`tests/run.sh`, `check-tools.sh`, `version.py check`, the isolation audit over `release-*.yml`, `scripts-release/`, `build-deb.yml` and `sdk-build.yml`), aggregate job named `pr-checks`, always runs, fails unless every job succeeded (a skipped job would satisfy a required check) |

Bookkeeping commits (single-parent, touching only `versions/` and the configured bookkeeping
files) are ignored by `unmerged` and the abandon check; the green-tip walk ignores
`versions/`-only commits (`walk-versions-only`). Merge commits never count as bookkeeping.

Merge-up is its own run, not a job of the RC build (ENG-2197). Before, a `merge-up` job in
`release-rc-build.yml` called `release-merge-up.yml` (`workflow_call`) after `publish-rc`, so a
merge-up conflict set the whole RC run's conclusion to `failure` although the RC was published,
and everything that asks "is this sha green" (`promote.sh`, `merge-up.sh` step 1) reads that
conclusion, so the sha stayed red for good (the reference hit it on ds-tegra-distro run
41505856957). Now `release-merge-up.yml` starts on `workflow_run` once an RC run concludes
`success`, and the RC run's conclusion is the RC's own: build, gate and publish. What to know
when reading its runs:

- `workflow_run` fires only for the copy of the file on the default branch, `main`; the RC run's
  own branch is what the `branches` filter sees. The filter is the two child-line shapes, so an RC
  on `main` creates no merge-up run; `resolve` still runs `lines.py` and skips (notice,
  `skip=true`) on a ref that is not a minor or patch line.
- `resolve` runs only when `workflow_run.conclusion == 'success'` and the App variable is set;
  with no App, `merge-up-skipped` prints the manual dispatch as a notice. A failed RC run creates
  a merge-up run whose jobs are all skipped.
- The child is `workflow_run.head_branch`. The merge-up run's own `head_branch`, `github.ref` and
  `github.sha` are `main`'s, so `gh run list --branch <child>` does not find it; `run-name` is
  `Merge-up <child>` for that reason.
- Stale sha: if `workflow_run.head_sha` is not among `walk-versions-only <child tip>` (the tip
  plus the `versions/`-only commits below it), a newer real commit landed and its own RC run
  will merge up, so `resolve` skips. A promote bump on top (`[skip ci]`, no RC run of its own)
  does not count as newer.
- `merge` passes `CALLER_SHA` (`workflow_run.head_sha`) and `CALLER_RUN` (`<workflow_run.id>
  <workflow_run.run_number>`, empty on a dispatch); `merge-up.sh` takes the tip as green without
  the `gh run list` lookup when it equals `CALLER_SHA` and `CALLER_RUN` is set. A dispatch, or a
  tip that moved since that sha (a versions-only bump), goes through the lookup, which reads the
  run **conclusion**: an RC run from before ENG-2197 whose in-run merge-up failed never counts
  as green; a new green RC on the line is needed first.
- An RC rerun that turns a failed run green completes again and fires merge-up then. Duplicate RC
  runs of one sha give two merge-ups; the second finds nothing to merge (the merge is idempotent).

The conflict PR (ENG-2200). When a file is still conflicted after `merge-keep-target`,
`merge-up.sh` commits the merge as it stands, conflict markers and all (`Merge <child> into
<parent> (conflicts to resolve)`, parents = the parent tip and the child tip; `versions/` and the
bookkeeping files already kept target-side), pushes it as `merge-up/<child>/<sha7>` and opens the
PR against `<parent>`, title `Merge-up <child> into <parent> (conflict)`, body listing the files
and how to resolve; `::error::` with the link, exit 1. The PR's diff is then parent-relative, so
the `<<<<<<<` blocks are the only lines to decide and the commit list is not the whole child
history. `pr-checks` on that branch goes red while the markers are in only when a marked file is
one the SDK build or the release-tooling tests read (ds has no lint that parses every file, so
markers in a doc or a comment leave it green); the marker check in `merge-up.sh` at `pr=N` is the
guard that always holds. A re-run leaves a branch that already exists on origin alone (a resolver
may have pushed to it; `git ls-remote` first) and reuses the open PR (`gh pr list` first). Before
ENG-2200 the branch was pushed at the child tip and the resolver had to merge the parent in to
see the conflicts. Resolving is: check out the branch, edit the marked files, commit, push, get the
review, dispatch with `pr=N`; if the parent moved first, merge it into the branch again.

Finishing (`pr=N`): the PR must be open, against `<parent>`, on `merge-up/<child>/*`; `gh pr view
--json reviewDecision,mergeStateStatus,statusCheckRollup,reviews,commits` goes through
`release_git.py pr-check` (`APPROVED`, an approval by a person other than the last commit's author,
bot reviews such as the Claude review workflow's ignored,
`mergeStateStatus == CLEAN`, no failed check). That read needs the token permissions `checks: read`
and `actions: read` together (`checks` for the check runs, `actions` for the workflow run behind
each; without `checks` it fails with `Resource not accessible by integration`; found 2026-11-10 on
ds-tegra-distro run 42899698997, details in that repo's `.ai/release-lines.md`), so the App token
step requests both. Then the PR head is fetched and `git grep` checks it
for `^<<<<<<< ` or `^>>>>>>> ` lines: any left means the resolver has not finished, `::error::`
naming the files, exit 1. Then `gh pr merge N --merge` and the post-check (second parent = the PR
head, child tip an ancestor). The PR path merges what the human left on the branch, `versions/`
included; that is the human's call. A dry run of a conflict prints the trial merge, its conflict
list and the branch it would push (parent plus the merge, markers in the listed files; nothing
pushed, no PR opened) and exits 0. Tests: `MergeUpTests` in `tests/test_release_git.py` and
`tests/test_merge_up.sh` (the clean merge, the conflict with the markers committed, the re-run,
the dry run, the refused and the accepted finish).

## The gate (`rc-gate.sh`, a rubber stamp until ENG-2139)

Per flavor, in S3 by run id: the newest prefix whose `manifest.json` names the flavor;
`buildparams.{product, build_version, flavor}` match the plan; `commit_hashes[name == <repo
name>].commit == sha`; every deb and extra entry has `key`, 64-hex `sha256`, integer `bytes`,
exists with that size, and `SHA256SUMS` agrees (its `manifest.json` line matches the download).
Then by `artifact.kind`:

- `debs` (this repo): the package set equals `artifact.debs` exactly (`ds-sensor-portal`);
  `architecture == deb_arch`; versions follow `deb_version_rule` (`deb-version`: equals
  `version.py deb-version <build_version>` with or without a `-r<N>` revision; `recipe-pv`:
  `^1\.0\+git`); the `version_probe` deb's binary contains `v<build_version>` (binary-safe grep).
- `install-trees` (ds): `artifacts.debs` empty, `artifacts.install_tree {key, services}` present
  with the tarball and the info file listed in `extra`, `services` equal to the config; the
  tarball is downloaded, hashes to its recorded sha256, its top level is exactly the configured
  services plus the info file, the info file says `ds_version=<build_version>` and
  `ds_commit=<sha>`, and the `version_probe` path read with `tar -xzOf` contains the pattern.

Both kinds copy `buildparams.sdk` into the build entry as `sdk`. `--kind release --rc-manifest
<the RC's release-manifest.json>` requires each build's `sdk` to equal the RC build's for the
same flavor (an RC that recorded none fails too: rerun its build). It writes
`release-manifest.json` (schema 1: `kind`, `product`, `version`, `pending`, `sha`, `line`,
`run_id`, `run_url`, `builds[].files = {debs, extra, manifest, sha256sums}`, `builds[].sdk`, plus
`rc_run` and `release_run` for `--kind release`), which publish-rc, promote, ship and prune-rc
read through the same file groups for both kinds (their tables say Artifacts, debs plus extra
files). Every miss is one `::error::`; all flavors are checked before exit 1.

## Scripts and tests

`scripts-release/`: `lib.sh` (annotations, `sha7`, `rc_tag`, `product_tag`, `product_label`,
`parse_flavor`, `json_field`, the S3 helpers, `GH_REPO_ARGS`, `gh_release_state`, `no_app_hint`,
`GIT_BOT_NAME`/`GIT_BOT_EMAIL` = `ds-os-release-bot[bot]`, `PRODUCT_PREFIX` read once from the
config), `version.py`, `lines.py`, `plan.py`, `release_git.py`, `artifacts.py` (`build-manifest
--kind debs|install-trees`, `--sdk-*`), `rc-gate.sh`, `publish-rc.sh`, `promote.sh`, `ship.sh`,
`create-branch.sh`, `merge-up.sh`, `abandon.sh`, `prune-rc.sh`, `check-tools.sh`. Signatures live
in each file's header comment. The directory is byte-identical in ds (the plan is one shared
tooling repo, ENG-2138): nothing in it may name this repo's layout, and a change here is copied
there. The scripts never touch the checkout's working tree: commits happen in worktrees and
temporary clones. `tests/run.sh` runs every `test_*.py` (`test_artifacts.py` among them) and
`test_*.sh` with stub `gh` and `aws` on PATH (`tests/stubs/`, `tests/stubs-tree/`: one bare repo,
trunk `main`), no network, no git identity; the gate, ship and publish-rc tests carry fixtures of
both artifact kinds (debs with `dpkg-deb`, tarballs with `tar`), so the suite is the same file
set as in ds; `pr-checks` runs it on every PR. A new test only needs the file.

## Pilot and go-live

The pilot runs live on `versions/portal = 0.0.1` with both rulesets already `active` and the
old `Default` ruleset disabled (it has no App bypass and would refuse the pilot's own bump push,
so `release-lines` replaces it before the first promote) and immutable releases off (the stray
tag `v0.0` is not `X.Y.Z` and never matches): first RC on `main`, promote `main` with
`bump=patch` (`v0.0.1`), `create-branch version=0.0.1 kind=patch` (`0.0.x`, pending `0.0.2`), a
PR into it (RC and automatic merge-up), a deliberate conflict finished with `pr=<n>`, promote
`0.0.x`, ship `v0.0.1` with Raul's approval, abandon `0.0.x`. Then the go-live PR sets
`versions/portal` to `4.0.0`, `prune-rc.sh --product portal --below 4.0.0` removes the pilot RCs
(the pilot releases stay as history), the one go-live switch flips (`docs/release-admin-setup.md`:
immutable releases), and `create-branch version=3.0.0 kind=minor seed_from=main` opens `3.x.x` at
`main`'s tip with `3.1.0` pending, so `3.x.x` and `main` start identical and `main` takes the 4.0
work. The hand-made `v3.0.0` tag is the 3.0.0 release as is (`bulbasaur`, the old shipping branch, sits on that
same commit with nothing past it); no `3.0.x` line is opened at go-live, and one can be opened
later from the tag (`version=3.0.0 kind=patch`) if a 3.0.1 is ever needed. `main`'s first release
is `v4.0.0`. The review bot in `pr-review.yml` only comments, so the ruleset's one approval
always comes from a person. Its `automated-review` job is skipped when a bot triggered the event
(`github.event.sender.type == 'Bot'`): `claude-code-action` refuses a non-human actor, so a
merge-up conflict PR the release bot opens used to get a failed check and no review. A person's
push to that PR still gets reviewed. The pilot builds against the
explicit `sdk.env` pin; when the first vertex release ships an SDK, one PR drops the pin lines
and the resolver takes over.

## Linear

ENG-2110 (this port), ENG-2126 (the SDK build this sits on), ENG-2138 (one shared tooling repo
instead of three copies, cost/benefit), ENG-2139 (real tests in the gate). The reference
implementation issues are ENG-1930 to ENG-1941 in ds-tegra-distro.
